Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum guaranteed paid fraction in cycles for any graph on 10 nodes (part a) and any non-complete graph (part b).

## Setting Up the Model

Each channel (edge) must have at least one free direction. To maximize paid transmissions, the administrators orient each edge: the chosen direction is free, the reverse is paid. A cycle's "paid fraction" counts edges traversed against the orientation.

**Key observation:** Any directed cycle (all-free) gives paid fraction 0, so the orientation must be **acyclic**. The problem reduces to: given graph $G$, find an acyclic orientation maximizing the minimum over all cycles $C$ of $\frac{\text{backward}(C)}{|C|}$.

## The Gallai–Hasse–Roy–Vitaver Theorem

This theorem states: the minimum over all acyclic orientations of the longest directed path length (in edges) equals $\chi(G) - 1$, where $\chi(G)$ is the chromatic number.

**Consequence:** In the optimal acyclic orientation, the longest directed path has $\chi(G) - 1$ edges ($\chi(G)$ vertices). Adding the "back-edge" from the path's end to its start creates a cycle with exactly **1 backward edge** and length $\chi(G)$, giving paid fraction $\frac{1}{\chi(G)}$.

## Part (a): $x_a$ — worst case over all graphs on 10 nodes

**Upper bound ($x_a \leq 1/10$):** Take $G = K_{10}$, with $\chi(K_{10}) = 10$. Every acyclic orientation of $K_{10}$ is a transitive tournament (total order). The Hamiltonian cycle $v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_{10}, v_1$ (in order) has 9 forward edges and 1 backward edge, giving paid fraction $1/10$. No acyclic orientation can avoid this, and any non-acyclic orientation has a directed cycle (fraction 0). So the best achievable is $1/10$.

**Lower bound ($x_a \geq 1/10$):** For any graph $G$ on 10 vertices, take any acyclic orientation. Every cycle has $\geq 1$ backward edge, and every cycle has length $\leq 10$, so the paid fraction is $\geq 1/10$.

Therefore $x_a = \frac{1}{10} = 10\%$.

## Part (b): $x_b$ — worst case over non-complete graphs on 10 nodes

**Any non-complete graph has $\chi \leq 9$:** If $G \neq K_{10}$, there exist non-adjacent vertices $u, v$ that can share a color, so $\chi(G) \leq 9$.

**Lower bound ($x_b \geq 1/9$):** For any non-complete $G$ with $\chi(G) \leq 9$, the optimal acyclic orientation has longest directed path $\leq 8$ edges. Any cycle with 1 backward edge has length $\leq 9$, giving fraction $\geq 1/9$. Cycles with $\geq 2$ backward edges have length $\leq 10$, giving fraction $\geq 2/10 = 1/5 > 1/9$. So every cycle has paid fraction $\geq 1/9$.

**Upper bound ($x_b \leq 1/9$):** Take $G = K_{10} \setminus \{1, 10\}$ (complete graph minus one edge). Then $\chi(G) = 9$ (the subgraph on $\{2,...,10\}$ is $K_9$, and we can 9-color by giving vertices 1 and 10 the same color). By the Gallai–Hasse–Roy–Vitaver theorem, every acyclic orientation has a directed path of $\geq 8$ edges (9 vertices). The endpoints of this path have different colors, so they are adjacent (the only non-edge $\{1,10\}$ connects same-colored vertices). This forms a cycle of length 9 with exactly 1 backward edge, giving paid fraction $1/9$. No orientation can avoid this.

Therefore $x_b = \frac{1}{9} = \frac{100}{9}\%$.

## Final Computation

$$x_a + x_b = 10 + \frac{100}{9} = \frac{90 + 100}{9} = \boxed{\dfrac{190}{9}}$$

